every incentive to waste  as much time as possible knowing that  the Senators only have five minutes for  each person despite the witness having  every incentive to waste time there are  plenty of tactics that you can use to  get the witness to answer yes or no now  first of all you have to ask a yes or no  question this doesn't work unless you  ask very pointed questions which a lot  of the Senators failed to do but if you  are phrasing your questions correctly in  the form of a yes-or-no question and the  witness is still non-responsive here's  an example of what you can do you can  say thank you for that answer but my  question was different my question was  and then just repeat your question and  then if the witness is still  non-responsive then you escalate it you  interrupt the witness and say I'm sorry  the question that I asked was yes or no  blank and then we ask the question now  if you have more time than you can try  and get the witness on your side by  explaining that you're trying to get an  answer to your question and that the  other side will have the opportunity to  allow you to expound on your answer but  you're just asking for yes or no  question  I don't want this to seem like it should  just be an incredibly aggressive process  it sort of depends on the witness but  there are a ton of rhetorical devices  that you can use to get yes or no  responses to accurately phrased  questions and here unfortunately I don't  think a lot of the Democratic senators  had much trial experience and didn't  have a lot of the rhetorical devices in  their pocket that they should have all  right so let's think like a lawyer in  regards to this question in other words  let's try to come up with the strongest  arguments on both sides of this issue I  think the Democrats would argue that  these answers don't comport with  anyone's reasonable expectation of these  particular words and the only  explanation is that judge Kavanagh is  trying to avoid the truth in order to  get through this hearing  they don't comport with common  experience and they are not accurate  representations of these words I think  that the Republican response would be  that the Democratic senators failed to  ask good cross-examination questions  they failed to pin down the witness they  allowed the witness to evade the  questions and give the answers that he  wanted to give and failed to pin down  the witness to a particular answer that  qualifies as the legal definition of  perjury now there is a separate question  as to whether a nominee should evade  questions and obfuscate but that brings  us to our second question did judge  Cavanaugh display a judge like  temperament now any judge holds a  special position in society they are the  arbiters of our disputes theoretically  they should all be impartial they need  to be able to hear both sides and make  judgment calls that have a huge effect  on the litigants lives now justices of  the Supreme Court are appointed for life  and they hold an even higher place in  our society they literally make  life-or-death decisions sometimes so  it's not surprising that both judges and  justices are held to a very very high  standard they are supposed to be neutral  they are not supposed to take a partisan  stance they are supposed to be able to  be the neutral arbiters of our general  disputes in his opening statement judge  Cavanaugh made some very provocative  statements about the backlash being a  result of revenge  inge from the clintons this whole  two-week effort has been a calculated  and orchestrated political hit fueled  with a parent pent-up anger about  President Trump and the 2016 election  fear that has been unfairly stoked about  my judicial record revenge on behalf of  the Clintons and millions of dollars in  money from outside left-wing opposition  groups this is a circus judge Cavanaugh  also got into a sparring match with  senator Klobuchar when she asked about  past drinking I think you've probably  had beer center and and so you're saying  there's never been a case where you  drink so much that you didn't remember  what happened the night before or part  of what happened that's you're asking  about yeah blackout I don't know have  you could you answer the question judge  I just so you that's not happened is  that your answer  yeah and I'm curious if you have I have  no drinking problem nor do i okay thank  you and judge Kavanagh also got into  some sparring matches with other  senators about some pointed questions  did the word Ralph you used your  question if you're gonna relate to  alcohol I like her for that I like beer  I don't know if you'd okay like beer  senator and what do you like to direct  one is Senator what do you I judge have  you so let's think like a lawyer I think  the Democrats would probably argue that  this shows a character for not being  impartial for taking partisan stances  and that his hotheadedness shows that he  doesn't have the temperament to be a  Supreme Court justice who we hold to a  very very high standard not to say  anything of the allegations of sexual  assault that have given rise to this  whole hearing I think the Republicans  would probably respond that he should  not have been asked these questions in  the first place that they weren't proper  to begin with and that he is  understandably angry for being put in  this position now I think we can make  another parallel to  some trial law as a rule of evidence  generally character evidence is not  admissible at trial but the main  exception is where the accused has made  character an issue and they usually do  that by calling a bunch of witnesses to  explain how good of a person they are  and how they never could have done the  underlying crime now here judge Kavanagh  has done something similar he has  essentially said that he was not a heavy  drinker he has never blacked out  he has never used sexual slang in the  context that he's alleged and he has  never engaged in inappropriate sexual  behavior in some ways if we were to draw  a parallel to a trial this has opened  the door to test his veracity for  truthfulness and also to show whether  these superlative statements that he's  making are true or not so do you think  the judge Kavanaugh displayed a judge  like behavior let me know I leave it to  you now that takes me to issue number  three would judge Kavanaugh  if elevated to the Supreme Court prevent  an investigation into the President or  the White House now a lot of pundits  have argued that judge Kavanaugh  in particular was nominated to the  Supreme Court because he holds an  idiosyncratic view that the sitting  president cannot be indicted civilly or  criminally a lot of this stems from a  law review article that he wrote in the  Minnesota Law Review called the  separation of powers during the 44th  presidency and beyond in that Law Review  piece judge Kavanaugh argued that  Congress should pass a law that while  the president is in office he should not  be subject to civil lawsuits or to  criminal investigation or prosecution  he explained that based on his  first-hand experience in the White House  he came to believe that the job of the  president is quote far more difficult  than any other civil position in  government end quote and he also says  that the President must be able to focus  on the tasks of being president and  shouldn't be distracted by civil or  criminal trials judge Kavanagh  recognized the irony of his position  given that he started his legal career  in the independent counsel's office with  Kenneth Starr during the Clinton  impeachment investigation and he imposed  exactly the kinds of burdens that he's  saying a sitting president should not  have to deal with so let's think like a  lawyer about the  position I think the Republicans would  argue that the Supreme Court is supposed  to decide what is mandated by the  Constitution and they should not let  their individual policy preferences  guide their decision-making a Supreme  Court justice is supposed to interpret  the Constitution and not make policy by  Fiat and I think the Republicans would  argue that this Law Review piece is  advocating that Congress pass a law and  not that there is a constitutional  mandate for this particular policy  position and in fact judge Cavanaugh has  made statements to that effect that he  recognizes his policy preference is not  mentioned in the Constitution and may  contradict what's in the Constitution  and therefore his stance as a sitting  justice on the Supreme Court is not  informed by this particular Law Review  article or the policies that he  advocated I think the Democrats would  argue that while that's nice in theory  isn't it funny how often policy  preferences line up with one's own  personal theory of what the Constitution  mandates what it says and what needs to  be interpreted as a result of the  Constitution upon being elevated to the  Supreme Court they suddenly find a  textual reason for their particular  policy preference the bottom line is I  don't think we know for certain which  way judge Kavanaugh would rule if he was  elevated to the Supreme Court  you can argue both ways based on his  prior statements so that takes us to  question number four would dr. Christine  Blasi Ford's allegations of sexual  assault hold up in court and otherwise  could they lead to judge Kavanaugh being  convicted of sexual assault now criminal  law has the highest burden under the law  the prosecution in other words the  people making the claim must prove their  case beyond a reasonable doubt  now the accused technically has no  burden at all the prosecution doesn't  meet their burden then the defendant  should not have to make any showing in  order to avoid conviction so to decide  if the allegations would hold up in  court we need to look at the evidence  now there is one statement that I see  coming up in the news a lot that I think  we should talk about a lot of pundits  have argued that there is no evidence of  sexual assault  one of my biggest pet peeves as a lawyer  is when people will refer to  he said she said scenario and refer to  it as if there is no evidence even if  there is only he said she said with no  physical evidence there is in fact  evidence in the form of testimony  imagine if you had a police officer who  testified that she saw a bank robber  robbed a bank the bank robber could deny  that and technically that is a he-said  she-said scenario but I bet you would  probably think the first-hand testimony  of the police officer was pretty strong  evidence and in fact often you get  people talking about circumstantial  versus direct evidence and this is  another one of my big pet peeves  circumstantial evidence is evidence that  relies on an inference in order to  connect it to a conclusion of fact for  example DNA evidence at the scene of a  crime by contrast direct evidence  supports the truth of an assertion  directly without the need for additional  evidence or inference so I don't mean to  imply that circumstantial evidences ever  inherently bad or week often  circumstantial evidence is the strongest  evidence that there is but in this  particular case we happen to have direct  evidence we have dr. Christine Blasi  Ford's testimony now you may believe her  testimony or you may not believe her  testimony but if this were a court of  law that would still qualify as  particular evidence it would be direct  evidence in the form of her particular  first-hand testimony of what happened to  her in 1983  so as a threshold matter there is  evidence there is evidence on the side  of dr. Christine Blasi Ford in the form  of her testimony and there is evidence  on the side of Judge cabinet on the form  of his direct testimony and that is  separate from the question of whether  dr. Fords testimony would meet the  burden of beyond a reasonable doubt I  don't know how a jury would come out on  this question but as we discussed with